{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1165 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "b22e5e34c140e1a0002c0e61d2533016acb3bdcb6dbd94cc007b09a48736da96" :edb/db-before-t 1164 :edb/db-after-t 1165 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534477 50 #inst "2026-10-07T07:29:59.242Z" 13194139534477 true] [17592186157259 1020 #edb/ref 17592186290381 13194139534477 true] [17592186157259 1020 #edb/ref 17592186290395 13194139534477 true] [17592186157260 1020 #edb/ref 17592186290388 13194139534477 true] [17592186157260 1020 #edb/ref 17592186290403 13194139534477 true] [17592186157260 1020 #edb/ref 17592186290408 13194139534477 true] [17592186290381 1017 #edb/ref 17592186290382 13194139534477 true] [17592186290381 1089 "q-102970" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290381 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534477 true] [17592186290381 1100 "Solve the inequality ${log}_{2}⁡(3-2x)+3\\ge 0.$" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290381 1113 #uuid "811d98eb-aeee-5382-8ca0-3caca3e3ff71" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290381 1145 "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(3-2x)+3 & \\ge 0 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(3-2x) & \\ge -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $2.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(3-2x)} & \\ge {2}^{-3} \\\\ 3-2x & \\ge \\frac{1}{8} \\\\ -2x & \\ge -\\frac{23}{8} \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{23}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n3-2x>0\\,⇒\\,x<\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le \\frac{23}{16},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{23}{16}].$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x<\\frac{3}{2},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{2}).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{23}{16}]\\cap (-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{2})=(-\\infty ,\\frac{23}{16}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{23}{16}],$ which can also be written as $x\\le \\frac{23}{16}.$" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290382 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290383 13194139534477 true] [17592186290382 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290384 13194139534477 true] [17592186290382 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290385 13194139534477 true] [17592186290382 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290386 13194139534477 true] [17592186290382 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290387 13194139534477 true] [17592186290382 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534477 true] [17592186290382 1041 "selection" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290382 1104 #edb/ref 17592186290385 13194139534477 true] [17592186290382 1110 #uuid "2fff7798-1026-5c26-acd7-89c131212609" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290383 1034 "\\frac{23}{16}\\le x<\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290383 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534477 true] [17592186290383 1111 #uuid "864d4411-8284-525e-8ee2-32477d31b900" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290384 1034 "1\\le x<\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290384 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534477 true] [17592186290384 1111 #uuid "df2c3c7b-9d65-54ab-9eef-a9449dfacc2f" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290385 1034 "x\\le \\frac{23}{16}" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290385 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534477 true] [17592186290385 1111 #uuid "5e628c05-94d9-5a47-aca7-9fc97723ad32" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290386 1034 "x\\le \\frac{2}{3}" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290386 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534477 true] [17592186290386 1111 #uuid "0d6e4cda-cffc-5747-b05b-84c427a5cb57" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290387 1034 "0\\le x\\le \\frac{23}{16}" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290387 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534477 true] [17592186290387 1111 #uuid "f5b3c92c-0767-57b7-b3ca-e1a0d453eb94" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290388 1017 #edb/ref 17592186290389 13194139534477 true] [17592186290388 1089 "q-102982" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290388 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534477 true] [17592186290388 1100 "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{log}⁡(4x+7)>\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x-5).$" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290388 1113 #uuid "31b64f03-5dc6-5a5a-b015-b94acd6a2503" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290388 1145 "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $10.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4x+7)} & >{10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x-5)} \\\\ 4x+7 & >2x-5 \\\\ 2x & >-12 \\\\ x & >-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+7 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >-\\frac{7}{4} \\\\ 2x-5 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x>-6,$ which can be expressed as $(-6,\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>-\\frac{7}{4}$ and $x>\\frac{5}{2},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\frac{7}{4},\\infty )$ and $(\\frac{5}{2},\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-6,\\infty )\\cap (-\\frac{7}{4},\\infty )\\cap (\\frac{5}{2},\\infty )=(\\frac{5}{2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (\\frac{5}{2},\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x>\\frac{5}{2}.$" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290389 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290390 13194139534477 true] [17592186290389 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290391 13194139534477 true] [17592186290389 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290392 13194139534477 true] [17592186290389 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290393 13194139534477 true] [17592186290389 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290394 13194139534477 true] [17592186290389 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534477 true] [17592186290389 1041 "selection" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290389 1104 #edb/ref 17592186290390 13194139534477 true] [17592186290389 1110 #uuid "f9d0