10 & <4 \\\\ 5x & <14 \\\\ x & <\\frac{14}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n5x-10>0\\,⇒\\,x>2\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x<\\frac{14}{5},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{14}{5}).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>2,$ which can be expressed as $(2,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{14}{5})\\cap (2,\\infty )=(2,\\frac{14}{5})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (2,\\frac{14}{5}),$ which can also be written as $2<x<\\frac{14}{5}.$" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290396 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290397 13194139534477 true] [17592186290396 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290398 13194139534477 true] [17592186290396 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534477 true] [17592186290396 1041 "field-1" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290396 1104 #edb/ref 17592186290397 13194139534477 true] [17592186290396 1110 #uuid "9572af10-673e-5248-b371-a675c032ddc1" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290397 1034 "2" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290397 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534477 true] [17592186290397 1111 #uuid "fa09b022-0e31-53c0-b268-2f9ac542a0d6" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290398 1034 "0" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290398 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534477 true] [17592186290398 1111 #uuid "78d77802-a8b9-5376-b396-6938c960af5c" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290399 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290400 13194139534477 true] [17592186290399 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534477 true] [17592186290399 1041 "field-2" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290399 1104 #edb/ref 17592186290400 13194139534477 true] [17592186290399 1110 #uuid "a2436dff-3b4d-591d-b010-41224a182980" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290400 1034 "<" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290400 1103 #edb/ref 1759218