an be expressed as $[\\frac{{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-2},\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>1$ and $x>0,$ which can be expressed as $(1,\\infty )$ and $(0,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n[\\frac{{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-2},\\infty )\\cap (1,\\infty )\\cap (0,\\infty )=[\\frac{{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [\\frac{{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-2},\\infty ),$ which can also be written as \n\n$$\nx\\ge \\frac{{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-2}.\n$$" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290404 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290405 13194139534477 true] [17592186290404 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534477 true] [17592186290404 1041 "field-1" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290404 1104 #edb/ref 17592186290405 13194139534477 true] [17592186290404 1110 #uuid "d36b71b8-27d9-5c83-b18f-a7e844cf7903" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290405 1034 "\\ge" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290405 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534477 true] [17592186290405 1111 #uuid "9401a0c7-7cd0-5aa5-a3f7-63666ab7f52a" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290406 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290407 13194139534477 true] [17592186290406 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534477 true] [17592186290406 1041 "field-2" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290406 1104 #edb/ref 17592186290407 13194139534477 true] [17592186290406 1110 #uuid "91f66cdf-edae-5f03-a0dc-b76e20c873cb" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290407 1034 "\\frac{e^4}{e^4-2}" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290407 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534477 true] [17592186290407 1111 #uuid "da464385-3767-52ac-8bcb-424dc9f2e784" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290408 1017 #edb/ref 17592186290409 13194139534477 true] [17592186290408 1089 "q-88017" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290408 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534477 true] [17592186290408 1100 "Solve the inequality ${log}_{2}⁡(x-3)+3>{log}_{2}⁡(4x).$" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290408 1113 #uuid "945cc19b-dd63-5c46-b14b-c6f27b95b038" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290408 1145 "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $2.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x-3)+3} & >{2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(4x)} \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x-3)}\\cdot {2}^{3} & >{2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(4x)} \\\\ (x-3)\\cdot 8 & >4x \\\\ 8x-24 & >4x \\\\ 4x & >24 \\\\ x & >6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >3 \\\\ 4x & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x>6,$ which can be expressed as $(6,\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>3$ and $x>0,$ which can be expressed as $(3,\\infty )$ and $(0,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(6,\\infty )\\cap (3,\\infty )\\cap (0,\\infty )=(6,\\infty )\n$$\n\n Therefore, the solution is $x\\in (6,\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x>6.$" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290409 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290410 13194139534477 true] [17592186290409 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290411 13194139534477 true] [17592186290409 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290412 13194139534477 true] [17592186290409 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290413 13194139534477 true] [17592186290409 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290414 13194139534477 true] [17592186290409 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534477 true] [17592186290409 1041 "selection" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290409 1104 #edb/ref 17592186290411 13194139534477 true] [17592186290409 1110 #uuid "d036221f-dae9-5d74-bb25-c0a3daacb369" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290410 1034 "0<x<6" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290410 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534477 true] [17592186290410 1111 #uuid "010c4e33-71e9-578e-b16f-d921e626eaaf" 13194139534477 true] [17592186290411 1034 "x>6" 1